{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GirAohbKkNp3PRKH3n6Tqj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## CONTEXT\n2\t\n3\tProject `AR-S2J2J.R` constructs prediction surfaces over charge and distance for component peak particle velocity (PPV) and component peak particle acceleration (PPA). Recorded blast vibration time histories are then scaled to match predicted component peak accelerations to produce scenario waveform products. The research gap is specific: whether component-wise or multivariate modelling and component-peak scaling have support in blast-vibration literature, and how to state the associated limitations accurately. The target output is an English technical report.\n4\t\n5\t## SLOTS\n6\t\n7\t### SLOT 1: Treatment of component PPV/PPA responses in blast-vibration literature\n8\t\n9\tReview how blast-vibration literature treats component PPV and PPA responses: whether they are modelled separately by component (e.g., transverse, vertical, longitudinal), jointly as correlated responses, or summarised through vector or resultant peak quantities. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n10\t\n11\t### SLOT 2: Methods for multivariate or constrained response prediction\n12\t\n13\tIdentify the methods and diagnostics used for multivariate or constrained response prediction when blast vibration components are correlated or linked by a vector quantity. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n14\t\n15\t### SLOT 3: Literature precedent for component-peak scaling of recorded time histories\n16\t\n17\tAssess whether the blast-vibration literature contains precedents for scaling recorded blast vibration time histories to match predicted component peak accelerations or velocities. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n18\t\n19\t### SLOT 4: Description of scenario scaling to distinguish from related methods\n20\t\n21\tDetermine how scenario scaling of recorded blast time histories to predicted component peaks should be characterised so that it is not confused with spectral matching, target-spectrum adjustment, or synthetic blast waveform simulation. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n22\t\n23\t### SLOT 5: Limitations of peak-component scaling relative to physics-based source models\n24\t\n25\tIdentify the limitations that should be stated when scenario waveforms are scaled by peak component response rather than generated from a physics-based blast source model. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n26\t\n27\t## CONSTRAINTS\n28\t\n29\t- The output is written in English.\n30\t- Scope is restricted to blast-vibration literature; generic scaled-distance attenuation overviews that do not address the component-wise or multivariate modelling gap are outside scope.\n31\t- The subject is component-wise responses (PPV and PPA by axis), not resultant or vector peak quantities alone.\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"80a9db5f-5afd-493c-b628-70a5730ff352","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:49:40.678Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/md/gap-component-attenuation-and-scaling.prompt.md","content":"## CONTEXT\n\nProject `AR-S2J2J.R` constructs prediction surfaces over charge and distance for component peak particle velocity (PPV) and component peak particle acceleration (PPA). Recorded blast vibration time histories are then scaled to match predicted component peak accelerations to produce scenario waveform products. The research gap is specific: whether component-wise or multivariate modelling and component-peak scaling have support in blast-vibration literature, and how to state the associated limitations accurately. The target output is an English technical report.\n\n## SLOTS\n\n### SLOT 1: Treatment of component PPV/PPA responses in blast-vibration literature\n\nReview how blast-vibration literature treats component PPV and PPA responses: whether they are modelled separately by component (e.g., transverse, vertical, longitudinal), jointly as correlated responses, or summarised through vector or resultant peak quantities. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 2: Methods for multivariate or constrained response prediction\n\nIdentify the methods and diagnostics used for multivariate or constrained response prediction when blast vibration components are correlated or linked by a vector quantity. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 3: Literature precedent for component-peak scaling of recorded time histories\n\nAssess whether the blast-vibration literature contains precedents for scaling recorded blast vibration time histories to match predicted component peak accelerations or velocities. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 4: Description of scenario scaling to distinguish from related methods\n\nDetermine how scenario scaling of recorded blast time histories to predicted component peaks should be characterised so that it is not confused with spectral matching, target-spectrum adjustment, or synthetic blast waveform simulation. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 5: Limitations of peak-component scaling relative to physics-based source models\n\nIdentify the limitations that should be stated when scenario waveforms are scaled by peak component response rather than generated from a physics-based blast source model. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n## CONSTRAINTS\n\n- The output is written in English.\n- Scope is restricted to blast-vibration literature; generic scaled-distance attenuation overviews that do not address the component-wise or multivariate modelling gap are outside scope.\n- The subject is component-wise responses (PPV and PPA by axis), not resultant or vector peak quantities alone.\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
