{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QJXxKo8rhp3civdwZeJaLf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration component PPV PPA transverse vertical longitudinal attenuation prediction literature\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X\"},{\"title\":\"1 NEW PREDICTION MODEL OF BLAST-INDUCED PPV BASED ON\",\"url\":\"https://geomatejournal.com/geomate/article/download/4024/3311/7921\"},{\"title\":\"Propagation characteristics and prediction of blast-induced vibration on closely spaced rock tunnels - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0886779822000566\"},{\"title\":\"PPV and Frequency Characteristics of Tunnel Blast‐Induced Vibrations on Tunnel Surfaces and Tunnel Entrance Slope Faces - Wang - 2021 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2021/5527115\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"Predicting ground vibration during rock blasting using relevance vector machine improved with dual kernels and metaheuristic algorithms | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70939-w\"}]\n\nI've found relevant literature on blast vibration components, PPV, PPA, and attenuation prediction. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Vibration Components Analyzed:**\nData from the three directions of the blast—vertical, longitudinal, and transversal—are analyzed to determine the seismic wave attenuation. The data of vibration generated include peak particle velocity (PPV), peak particle acceleration (PPA), peak displacement, and frequency.\n\n**Attenuation Prediction:**\nCommon empirical attenuation equations represent prediction models for PPV as a function of scaled distance. The widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, and other factors are also considered in various empirical equations developed by various researchers.\n\n**Challenges in Prediction:**\nPPV does not decline uniformly with increasing distance for any one shot because of different geological conditions at related locations, and cannot attenuate with distance uniformly even for the designated blast due to near-surface low-geological layers. Six factors play significant roles in determining frequency attenuation, including rock density, P-wave velocity, charge per delay, vertical distance, direct distance and PPV.\n\nThe search returned recent literature (2020-2024) addressing these topics from both field testing and numerical simulation approaches.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"7ee59d9e-8412-416d-b977-b9a173108cdb","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:22.097Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration component PPV PPA transverse vertical longitudinal attenuation prediction literature","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_016d5ifw1NzwLMYujijGjKPE","content":[{"title":"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X"},{"title":"1 NEW PREDICTION MODEL OF BLAST-INDUCED PPV BASED ON","url":"https://geomatejournal.com/geomate/article/download/4024/3311/7921"},{"title":"Propagation characteristics and prediction of blast-induced vibration on closely spaced rock tunnels - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0886779822000566"},{"title":"PPV and Frequency Characteristics of Tunnel Blast‐Induced Vibrations on Tunnel Surfaces and Tunnel Entrance Slope Faces - Wang - 2021 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2021/5527115"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X"},{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"Predicting ground vibration during rock blasting using relevance vector machine improved with dual kernels and metaheuristic algorithms | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70939-w"}]},"I've found relevant literature on blast vibration components, PPV, PPA, and attenuation prediction. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Vibration Components Analyzed:**\nData from the three directions of the blast—vertical, longitudinal, and transversal—are analyzed to determine the seismic wave attenuation. The data of vibration generated include peak particle velocity (PPV), peak particle acceleration (PPA), peak displacement, and frequency.\n\n**Attenuation Prediction:**\nCommon empirical attenuation equations represent prediction models for PPV as a function of scaled distance. The widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, and other factors are also considered in various empirical equations developed by various researchers.\n\n**Challenges in Prediction:**\nPPV does not decline uniformly with increasing distance for any one shot because of different geological conditions at related locations, and cannot attenuate with distance uniformly even for the designated blast due to near-surface low-geological layers. Six factors play significant roles in determining frequency attenuation, including rock density, P-wave velocity, charge per delay, vertical distance, direct distance and PPV.\n\nThe search returned recent literature (2020-2024) addressing these topics from both field testing and numerical simulation approaches."],"durationSeconds":5.766116542000003,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NZaQZe5HwTiS8Ay6UdGpYS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration peak particle velocity component-wise model scaled distance attenuation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Prediction of Ground Vibration Velocity Induced by Long Hole Blasting Using a Particle Swarm Optimization Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/14/9/3839\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"Simulation of blast-induced ground vibrations using a machine learning-assisted mechanical framework | Environmental Earth Sciences | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-023-11194-6\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003\"},{\"title\":\"Discussion on the Discreteness of the Attenuation Parameters of the Peak Particle Velocity Induced by Blasting - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10934757/\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Discussion on the Discreteness of the Attenuation Parameters of the Peak Particle Velocity Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10934757/\"},{\"title\":\"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/\"}]\n\nI found relevant research on blast vibration modeling and prediction. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Peak Particle Velocity Models\n\nThe peak particle velocity (PPV) is primarily influenced by factors including the maximum charge per delay (Q), the distance from the blasting working face to the test point (R), the scaled distance (SD), and the site attenuation coefficients (k and α).\n\n## Scaled Distance Approach\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, with various empirical equations developed by different researchers. Research has shown that PPV fits the scaled distance with either cube root or square root scaling for the explosive quantity, depending on the specific conditions.\n\n## Component-Wise Analysis\n\nA typical blasting vibration wave is a composite wave, and its attenuation law is affected by the type of dominant wave component, with attenuation equations established taking into account the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves. The attenuation parameters of different types of waves are significantly different, and the study of attenuation law with only coupled particle peak vibration velocity often showed high discreteness.\n\n## Prediction Challenges\n\nDiscrepancies of the scaled distance method in predicting the peak particle velocity occur due to complex geology and the need for an extensive trial blast database.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"ead76a0e-e9e5-437e-91bd-4a2cc1a5d18f","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:24.199Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration peak particle velocity component-wise model scaled distance attenuation","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01MirdqWGmpV5A1VzPpVfG9G","content":[{"title":"Prediction of Ground Vibration Velocity Induced by Long Hole Blasting Using a Particle Swarm Optimization Algorithm","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/14/9/3839"},{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"Simulation of blast-induced ground vibrations using a machine learning-assisted mechanical framework | Environmental Earth Sciences | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-023-11194-6"},{"title":"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003"},{"title":"Discussion on the Discreteness of the Attenuation Parameters of the Peak Particle Velocity Induced by Blasting - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10934757/"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Discussion on the Discreteness of the Attenuation Parameters of the Peak Particle Velocity Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10934757/"},{"title":"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/"}]},"I found relevant research on blast vibration modeling and prediction. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Peak Particle Velocity Models\n\nThe peak particle velocity (PPV) is primarily influenced by factors including the maximum charge per delay (Q), the distance from the blasting working face to the test point (R), the scaled distance (SD), and the site attenuation coefficients (k and α).\n\n## Scaled Distance Approach\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, with various empirical equations developed by different researchers. Research has shown that PPV fits the scaled distance with either cube root or square root scaling for the explosive quantity, depending on the specific conditions.\n\n## Component-Wise Analysis\n\nA typical blasting vibration wave is a composite wave, and its attenuation law is affected by the type of dominant wave component, with attenuation equations established taking into account the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves. The attenuation parameters of different types of waves are significantly different, and the study of attenuation law with only coupled particle peak vibration velocity often showed high discreteness.\n\n## Prediction Challenges\n\nDiscrepancies of the scaled distance method in predicting the peak particle velocity occur due to complex geology and the need for an extensive trial blast database."],"durationSeconds":7.018735833999999,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TgBuVY7iAwpixY8aYAXdKh","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"multivariate blast vibration prediction correlated components covariance regression\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using multivariate\",\"url\":\"https://www.ias.ac.in/public/Volumes/sadh/049/00/0141.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Principal Component Analysis–Based Classification and Logarithmic Regression Technique | Mining, Metallurgy & Exploration | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42461-022-00659-0\"},{\"title\":\"Application of multivariate analysis for prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257101904_Application_of_multivariate_analysis_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibrations\"},{\"title\":\"Multivariate statistical analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration | Arabian Journal of Geosciences | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12517-018-3796-8\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of the ground vibration during blasting in mining projects using different computational approaches | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-46064-5\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration at a Limestone Quarry: An Artificial Intelligence Approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/108714106/Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Ground_Vibration_at_a_Limestone_Quarry_An_Artificial_Intelligence_Approach\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation and Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) Approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-6489/3/4/36\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant research on multivariate blast vibration prediction. Here's what the literature reveals:\n\n## Multivariate Approaches to Blast Vibration Prediction\n\nGround vibration is a major hazard from rock-blasting operations, and accurate prediction is necessary for designing controlled blasting parameters.\n\n### Regression Techniques\n\nResearch has experimented with different statistical approaches including linear, multiple, polynomial, and logarithmic regression-based models involving multiple influencing variables for predicting blast-induced ground vibration. Methods to determine peak particle velocity include USBM predictor methods and multivariate regression analysis.\n\n### Addressing Correlated Components\n\nPrincipal component analysis is a dimension reduction technique that has been used along with multivariate logarithmic regression to predict ground vibration. This approach is particularly useful for handling correlated variables.\n\nMulticollinearity can affect the reliability of statistical inferences in regression analysis when there is a high correlation between independent variables. Research shows that multicollinearity highly affects the overfitting of covariance function-based models like Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) and kernel function-based models like LSSVM.\n\n### Blast Parameters\n\nBlast design parameters like hole diameter, hole depth, explosive charge, number of blast holes, burden, spacing, and initiation system are controllable parameters affecting ground vibration.\n\nThe research indicates that handling correlated components through dimension reduction techniques and accounting for covariance relationships is important for robust multivariate prediction models in blast vibration assessment.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"8117a94f-749e-44a1-b0ab-b553262459e0","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:24.288Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"multivariate blast vibration prediction correlated components covariance regression","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01NGpMkWtL2tTfBVZorA6ask","content":[{"title":"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using multivariate","url":"https://www.ias.ac.in/public/Volumes/sadh/049/00/0141.pdf"},{"title":"Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Principal Component Analysis–Based Classification and Logarithmic Regression Technique | Mining, Metallurgy & Exploration | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42461-022-00659-0"},{"title":"Application of multivariate analysis for prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257101904_Application_of_multivariate_analysis_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibrations"},{"title":"Multivariate statistical analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration | Arabian Journal of Geosciences | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12517-018-3796-8"},{"title":"Assessment of the ground vibration during blasting in mining projects using different computational approaches | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-46064-5"},{"title":"(PDF) Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration at a Limestone Quarry: An Artificial Intelligence Approach","url":"https://www.academia.edu/108714106/Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Ground_Vibration_at_a_Limestone_Quarry_An_Artificial_Intelligence_Approach"},{"title":"Evaluation and Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) Approach","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-6489/3/4/36"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant research on multivariate blast vibration prediction. Here's what the literature reveals:\n\n## Multivariate Approaches to Blast Vibration Prediction\n\nGround vibration is a major hazard from rock-blasting operations, and accurate prediction is necessary for designing controlled blasting parameters.\n\n### Regression Techniques\n\nResearch has experimented with different statistical approaches including linear, multiple, polynomial, and logarithmic regression-based models involving multiple influencing variables for predicting blast-induced ground vibration. Methods to determine peak particle velocity include USBM predictor methods and multivariate regression analysis.\n\n### Addressing Correlated Components\n\nPrincipal component analysis is a dimension reduction technique that has been used along with multivariate logarithmic regression to predict ground vibration. This approach is particularly useful for handling correlated variables.\n\nMulticollinearity can affect the reliability of statistical inferences in regression analysis when there is a high correlation between independent variables. Research shows that multicollinearity highly affects the overfitting of covariance function-based models like Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) and kernel function-based models like LSSVM.\n\n### Blast Parameters\n\nBlast design parameters like hole diameter, hole depth, explosive charge, number of blast holes, burden, spacing, and initiation system are controllable parameters affecting ground vibration.\n\nThe research indicates that handling correlated components through dimension reduction techniques and accounting for covariance relationships is important for robust multivariate prediction models in blast vibration assessment."],"durationSeconds":6.6778390830000065,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019TsxmDBi9DA811i4J8X8kJ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration scenario waveform scaling limitations physics-based source model comparison\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017\"},{\"title\":\"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Near-field blast vibration models\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/284509528_Near-field_blast_vibration_models\"},{\"title\":\"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888\"},{\"title\":\"The Effect of Scaling Building Configuration Blast Experiments on Positive Phase Blast Wave Parameters\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/13/10/5956\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547\"},{\"title\":\"blast effects simulations: Topics by Science.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.science.gov/topicpages/b/blast+effects+simulations\"}]\n\nI've completed a web search on blast vibration scenario waveform scaling limitations and physics-based source model comparison. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Waveform Scaling Limitations\n\nThe charge weight scaling law does not contain waveform information and hence cannot provide any rational means of estimating the total peak vibration level from the sum of waveforms. Traditional charge weight scaling laws used to predict the peak vibration levels of a blast are plagued with many problems, including being dimensionally awkward and inconsistent with any notion of superposition.\n\n## Physics-Based Source Model Approaches\n\nResearch provides a comprehensive review of approaches to model the blasting source, discussing several approaches to analytically model explosives, ground vibration prediction methods focused on seed waveform and charge weight scaling techniques, and numerical simulations of the blasting source including methods to estimate blasthole wall pressure time-history and hydrodynamic codes.\n\n## Waveform Analysis and Simulation Methods\n\nThe common time-domain superposition method has limitations in accurately controlling spectrum characteristics, and the simulation research of frequency-domain waveforms remains insufficient. However, research proposes an amplitude-phase joint waveform simulation method based on Fourier analysis to meet the needs of blasting vibration analysis and safety assessment.\n\nThe assumption of linear time-invariant systems inherently limits prediction accuracy when applied to the nonlinear characteristics exhibited by blasting vibrations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"e5952399-9add-4c3a-9714-1835a23dbbf4","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:26.452Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration scenario waveform scaling limitations physics-based source model comparison","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01RUZqryTU6gN3ZuWtTYENvG","content":[{"title":"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017"},{"title":"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023"},{"title":"(PDF) Near-field blast vibration models","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/284509528_Near-field_blast_vibration_models"},{"title":"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888"},{"title":"The Effect of Scaling Building Configuration Blast Experiments on Positive Phase Blast Wave Parameters","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/13/10/5956"},{"title":"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547"},{"title":"blast effects simulations: Topics by Science.gov","url":"https://www.science.gov/topicpages/b/blast+effects+simulations"}]},"I've completed a web search on blast vibration scenario waveform scaling limitations and physics-based source model comparison. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Waveform Scaling Limitations\n\nThe charge weight scaling law does not contain waveform information and hence cannot provide any rational means of estimating the total peak vibration level from the sum of waveforms. Traditional charge weight scaling laws used to predict the peak vibration levels of a blast are plagued with many problems, including being dimensionally awkward and inconsistent with any notion of superposition.\n\n## Physics-Based Source Model Approaches\n\nResearch provides a comprehensive review of approaches to model the blasting source, discussing several approaches to analytically model explosives, ground vibration prediction methods focused on seed waveform and charge weight scaling techniques, and numerical simulations of the blasting source including methods to estimate blasthole wall pressure time-history and hydrodynamic codes.\n\n## Waveform Analysis and Simulation Methods\n\nThe common time-domain superposition method has limitations in accurately controlling spectrum characteristics, and the simulation research of frequency-domain waveforms remains insufficient. However, research proposes an amplitude-phase joint waveform simulation method based on Fourier analysis to meet the needs of blasting vibration analysis and safety assessment.\n\nThe assumption of linear time-invariant systems inherently limits prediction accuracy when applied to the nonlinear characteristics exhibited by blasting vibrations."],"durationSeconds":7.443663166999999,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TR2g1wQ6S4gXPwNP7ZBjF9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration waveform scaling recorded time history peak acceleration velocity matching\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Relationship between peak particle acceleration, velocity and displacement of blast vibration\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392403274_Relationship_between_peak_particle_acceleration_velocity_and_displacement_of_blast_vibration\"},{\"title\":\"Research on the Prediction of Blasting Vibration Velocity in Open Pit Mines Based on the SSA-BP Model - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12676330/\"},{\"title\":\"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...\",\"url\":\"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Blast Vibration Basics Post #2 — Thoroughbred Drill and Blast Consultants\",\"url\":\"https://tbredblast.com/posts/blastvibration2\"},{\"title\":\"Vibration velocity and frequency characteristics of surrounding rock of adjacent tunnel under blasting excavation | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-12203-7\"},{\"title\":\"MANAGING THE IMPACTS OF BLAST-INDUCED VIBRATION AND OVERPRESSURE ON FISH\",\"url\":\"https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/clas/pdfs/fhwa-flh-td_19001.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Full Waveform Prediction of Blasting Vibration Using Deep Learning\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/14/13/8200\"},{\"title\":\"Blasting Operations Using Different Initiation Methods in Deep   Underground Mines\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.02125\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query. Here are the main findings related to blast vibration waveform scaling, recorded time history, and peak acceleration/velocity matching:\n\n## Key Information Found:\n\n**Recording and Processing of Blast Vibrations:**\nBlast vibration waveforms are recorded using accelerometers in the field, and recorded particle acceleration waveforms are integrated to obtain particle velocity waveforms, which are then integrated into displacement waveforms.\n\n**Peak Measurements:**\nPeak particle acceleration (PPA) is defined as the largest absolute particle acceleration in the waveform, while PPV (peak particle velocity) or PPD (peak particle displacement) are defined similarly from particle velocity or displacement waveforms.\n\n**Acceleration-Velocity Relationship:**\nDisplacement and acceleration time histories are simply the integration and differentiation of the velocity time history, respectively, and the peak over time is reported.\n\n**Time-History Analysis:**\nTime-history curves of vibration velocity at different blast-center distances are obtained along with their corresponding peak vibration velocities. Results of time-history curves and frequency spectra can be compared to verify the accuracy of numerical models.\n\n**Frequency Consideration:**\nBoth displacement and acceleration are highly influenced by the frequency content of the entire velocity pulse and not necessarily just the frequency at the peak.\n\nThe search results contain technical information on how blast vibration waveforms are recorded, processed, and analyzed for peak values and frequency characteristics.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"883a7719-96b1-43dc-abcf-19b3f39006aa","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:28.695Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration waveform scaling recorded time history peak acceleration velocity matching","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Tp538iECLfeXazPDypQ1or","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Relationship between peak particle acceleration, velocity and displacement of blast vibration","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392403274_Relationship_between_peak_particle_acceleration_velocity_and_displacement_of_blast_vibration"},{"title":"Research on the Prediction of Blasting Vibration Velocity in Open Pit Mines Based on the SSA-BP Model - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12676330/"},{"title":"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...","url":"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf"},{"title":"Blast Vibration Basics Post #2 — Thoroughbred Drill and Blast Consultants","url":"https://tbredblast.com/posts/blastvibration2"},{"title":"Vibration velocity and frequency characteristics of surrounding rock of adjacent tunnel under blasting excavation | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-12203-7"},{"title":"MANAGING THE IMPACTS OF BLAST-INDUCED VIBRATION AND OVERPRESSURE ON FISH","url":"https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/clas/pdfs/fhwa-flh-td_19001.pdf"},{"title":"Full Waveform Prediction of Blasting Vibration Using Deep Learning","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/14/13/8200"},{"title":"Blasting Operations Using Different Initiation Methods in Deep   Underground Mines","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.02125"}]},"I've completed the web search for your query. Here are the main findings related to blast vibration waveform scaling, recorded time history, and peak acceleration/velocity matching:\n\n## Key Information Found:\n\n**Recording and Processing of Blast Vibrations:**\nBlast vibration waveforms are recorded using accelerometers in the field, and recorded particle acceleration waveforms are integrated to obtain particle velocity waveforms, which are then integrated into displacement waveforms.\n\n**Peak Measurements:**\nPeak particle acceleration (PPA) is defined as the largest absolute particle acceleration in the waveform, while PPV (peak particle velocity) or PPD (peak particle displacement) are defined similarly from particle velocity or displacement waveforms.\n\n**Acceleration-Velocity Relationship:**\nDisplacement and acceleration time histories are simply the integration and differentiation of the velocity time history, respectively, and the peak over time is reported.\n\n**Time-History Analysis:**\nTime-history curves of vibration velocity at different blast-center distances are obtained along with their corresponding peak vibration velocities. Results of time-history curves and frequency spectra can be compared to verify the accuracy of numerical models.\n\n**Frequency Consideration:**\nBoth displacement and acceleration are highly influenced by the frequency content of the entire velocity pulse and not necessarily just the frequency at the peak.\n\nThe search results contain technical information on how blast vibration waveforms are recorded, processed, and analyzed for peak values and frequency characteristics."],"durationSeconds":10.215606916000004,"searchCount":1}}
