{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015hMDuR9zyvvsHW4Yqw7SZp","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration transverse vertical longitudinal component separate prediction regression empirical\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using an Adaptive Network-Based Fuzzy Inference System\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/348028991_Prediction_of_Blast-Induced_Ground_Vibration_Using_an_Adaptive_Network-Based_Fuzzy_Inference_System\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Prediction of Blast induced Vibration- Statistical and Simulation Approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327263077_Prediction_of_Blast_induced_Vibration-_Statistical_and_Simulation_Approach\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/\"}]\n\nI found several relevant research articles on blast-induced vibration prediction. Here's a summary of the search results:\n\n## Key Research Findings:\n\nStudies analyze all three components of recorded velocity: the longitudinal, transversal and the vertical component. Blast-induced ground vibrations in three orthogonal directions (transverse, vertical, and longitudinal) are recorded at different distances using seismographs.\n\n## Prediction Approaches:\n\nCommon empirical attenuation equations represent prediction models for peak particle velocity (PPV) as a function of scaled distance. Multivariate logarithmic regression has been used to predict ground vibration, with regression analysis of principal component scores.\n\n## Research on Stochastic Nature:\n\nThe analysis reveals that recordings belong to a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs. An improved prediction model for peak particle velocity is proposed based on a neural network.\n\n## Empirical Prediction Equations:\n\nTwo new empirical prediction equations of blasting vibration propagation and attenuation were established: one for PPV and one for PPVi (maximum value of the three component velocities).\n\nThe research demonstrates that separate analysis and prediction of the three directional components is a standard approach in blast vibration prediction using both regression and machine learning methods.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"ca6ada85-bc10-4a64-907a-0c71a4756792","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:46.276Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration transverse vertical longitudinal component separate prediction regression empirical","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FKf1RzKg7NGD5ztoVuBYKu","content":[{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full"},{"title":"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting"},{"title":"(PDF) Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using an Adaptive Network-Based Fuzzy Inference System","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/348028991_Prediction_of_Blast-Induced_Ground_Vibration_Using_an_Adaptive_Network-Based_Fuzzy_Inference_System"},{"title":"(PDF) Prediction of Blast induced Vibration- Statistical and Simulation Approach","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327263077_Prediction_of_Blast_induced_Vibration-_Statistical_and_Simulation_Approach"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/"}]},"I found several relevant research articles on blast-induced vibration prediction. Here's a summary of the search results:\n\n## Key Research Findings:\n\nStudies analyze all three components of recorded velocity: the longitudinal, transversal and the vertical component. Blast-induced ground vibrations in three orthogonal directions (transverse, vertical, and longitudinal) are recorded at different distances using seismographs.\n\n## Prediction Approaches:\n\nCommon empirical attenuation equations represent prediction models for peak particle velocity (PPV) as a function of scaled distance. Multivariate logarithmic regression has been used to predict ground vibration, with regression analysis of principal component scores.\n\n## Research on Stochastic Nature:\n\nThe analysis reveals that recordings belong to a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs. An improved prediction model for peak particle velocity is proposed based on a neural network.\n\n## Empirical Prediction Equations:\n\nTwo new empirical prediction equations of blasting vibration propagation and attenuation were established: one for PPV and one for PPVi (maximum value of the three component velocities).\n\nThe research demonstrates that separate analysis and prediction of the three directional components is a standard approach in blast vibration prediction using both regression and machine learning methods."],"durationSeconds":7.333274416999993,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012h95tZHg4YdX17RFYw7Jg3","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast induced vibration resultant vector peak versus component peak particle velocity comparison\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Estimation of blast-induced peak particle velocity using ensemble machine learning algorithms: A case study\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361977990_Estimation_of_blast-induced_peak_particle_velocity_using_ensemble_machine_learning_algorithms_A_case_study\"},{\"title\":\"Resultant Peak Particle Velocity against Scaled Distance for the Charge... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Resultant-Peak-Particle-Velocity-against-Scaled-Distance-for-the-Charge-Sizes_fig1_274008170\"},{\"title\":\"3.2 Effects of Vibration on the Building Structure\",\"url\":\"http://www.environmental.co.uk/Chapter3.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"},{\"title\":\"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...\",\"url\":\"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Discussion on the Discreteness of the Attenuation Parameters of the Peak Particle Velocity Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10934757/\"},{\"title\":\"Peak particle velocity prediction using support vector machines: A surface blasting case study\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532015000700012\"},{\"title\":\"Determination of blast-induced ground vibration equations for rocks using mechanical and geological properties - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477551600024X\"},{\"title\":\"Determination of Blast Vibrations Using Peak Particle ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:1025939/FULLTEXT01.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about blast-induced vibration measurements comparing resultant vector peak and component peak particle velocity:\n\n## Key Distinctions\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) can be reported in three different ways: as the 'peak component particle velocity' (the maximum value in any direction), the 'peak true resultant particle velocity' (summing the three orthogonal components coincident with time), or the 'maximum vector sum' (vector sum using maximum of each component regardless of time).\n\n## Measurement Approaches\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is the maximum velocity in any of the components and peak vector sum (PVS) is the true vector sum of the three components.\n\nMore specifically, Peak vector sum (PVS) is the resultant particle component which means square root of addition of particle velocity of individual component. The mathematical expression is: PPV_R = √(ppvx² + ppvy²), where ppvx and ppvy are the component peak particle velocities along the X and Y coordinate axes.\n\n## Standards Consideration\n\nThe maximum vector sum format is now discouraged as it includes an unknown factor of safety, which suggests that standards have evolved to prefer more precise measurement methods.\n\nThe choice between these methods is important for accurately assessing blast-induced vibration impacts and ensuring proper structural safety evaluations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"845a5b21-10df-48ed-9b0f-16e9684406fc","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:46.965Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast induced vibration resultant vector peak versus component peak particle velocity comparison","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0148p7ULhukFSPoRgbni8c6U","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Estimation of blast-induced peak particle velocity using ensemble machine learning algorithms: A case study","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/361977990_Estimation_of_blast-induced_peak_particle_velocity_using_ensemble_machine_learning_algorithms_A_case_study"},{"title":"Resultant Peak Particle Velocity against Scaled Distance for the Charge... | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Resultant-Peak-Particle-Velocity-against-Scaled-Distance-for-the-Charge-Sizes_fig1_274008170"},{"title":"3.2 Effects of Vibration on the Building Structure","url":"http://www.environmental.co.uk/Chapter3.htm"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"},{"title":"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...","url":"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf"},{"title":"Discussion on the Discreteness of the Attenuation Parameters of the Peak Particle Velocity Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10934757/"},{"title":"Peak particle velocity prediction using support vector machines: A surface blasting case study","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532015000700012"},{"title":"Determination of blast-induced ground vibration equations for rocks using mechanical and geological properties - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477551600024X"},{"title":"Determination of Blast Vibrations Using Peak Particle ...","url":"https://www.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:1025939/FULLTEXT01.pdf"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"}]},"Based on the search results, here's information about blast-induced vibration measurements comparing resultant vector peak and component peak particle velocity:\n\n## Key Distinctions\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) can be reported in three different ways: as the 'peak component particle velocity' (the maximum value in any direction), the 'peak true resultant particle velocity' (summing the three orthogonal components coincident with time), or the 'maximum vector sum' (vector sum using maximum of each component regardless of time).\n\n## Measurement Approaches\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is the maximum velocity in any of the components and peak vector sum (PVS) is the true vector sum of the three components.\n\nMore specifically, Peak vector sum (PVS) is the resultant particle component which means square root of addition of particle velocity of individual component. The mathematical expression is: PPV_R = √(ppvx² + ppvy²), where ppvx and ppvy are the component peak particle velocities along the X and Y coordinate axes.\n\n## Standards Consideration\n\nThe maximum vector sum format is now discouraged as it includes an unknown factor of safety, which suggests that standards have evolved to prefer more precise measurement methods.\n\nThe choice between these methods is important for accurately assessing blast-induced vibration impacts and ensuring proper structural safety evaluations."],"durationSeconds":7.5556034579999976,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019iU4gg3mQm6yAojBHAiTZW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"scaled waveform blast seismic time history amplitude scaling ground motion spectral matching\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"In recent studies, spectral matching is the most commonly proposed method for selecting earthquake records for time-history analysis of structures. However, until now, there have been no serious investigations of the effects of coordinate values on the scaling of ground motions. This paper investigated the influence of using arithmetic and logarithmic values of response spectra in spectral matching procedures (i.e., ASM and LSM methods) on the results of nonlinear structural time-history analysis. Steel mom\",\"url\":\"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/sv/2020/8180612/\"},{\"title\":\"Selection and spectral matching of recorded ground motions for seismic fragility analyses | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-022-01393-0\"},{\"title\":\"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear drift responses? | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227933785_Does_amplitude_scaling_of_ground_motion_records_result_in_biased_nonlinear_drift_responses\"},{\"title\":\"On selection and scaling of ground motions for analysis of seismically isolated structures | Earthquake Engineering and Engineering Vibration | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11803-016-0354-9\"},{\"title\":\"Introducing a response-based duration metric and its correlation with   structural damages\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10604\"},{\"title\":\"Amplitude scaling of ground motions as a potential source of bias: Large‐scale investigations on structural drifts - Tsalouchidis - 2022 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/eqe.3707\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Time Histories\",\"url\":\"https://www.structuremag.org/article/seismic-time-histories-a-practical-approach/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Selection and Scaling Approaches of Earthquake Time‑Series for Structural Engineering Applications: A State‑of‑the‑Art Review\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/375888682_Selection_and_Scaling_Approaches_of_Earthquake_Time-Series_for_Structural_Engineering_Applications_A_State-of-the-Art_Review\"},{\"title\":\"NIST GCR 11-917-15 Selecting and Scaling Earthquake Ground\",\"url\":\"https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/nistgcr11-917-15hires.pdf\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query. Here are the key findings related to scaled waveform blast seismic time history amplitude scaling, ground motion spectral matching:\n\n## Key Concepts Found:\n\n**Spectral Matching Procedures:**\nSpectral matching is the most commonly proposed method for selecting earthquake records for time-history analysis of structures.\n\n**Amplitude Scaling Methods:**\nWeighted scaling, also commonly referred to as amplitude scaling or linear scaling, as well as spectral matching, are investigated for ground motion selection. The amplitude of ground motions can be scaled to match the mean spectrum of selected ground motions and the target spectrum.\n\n**How Spectral Matching Works:**\nEach Fourier spectral ordinate is subjected to a different scaling factor, the amplitude of which depends on the distance between the original response spectrum and the target one. Larger scaling factors are applied to the high-frequency portion, mostly governed by small-scale effects poorly correlated with the earthquake source.\n\n**Applications:**\nGiven the rarity of Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCE) level events, it is common to scale specific acceleration records to match an MCE level event for a given site.\n\n**Bias Considerations:**\nScaled records can be safely used in nonlinear response-history analysis to assess the seismic structural behaviour if spectral and scenario compatibility are ensured and it is verified that the sustained amplitude is also consistent.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"da82fd89-95a1-4c8c-81a3-a0203c967d90","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:47.320Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"scaled waveform blast seismic time history amplitude scaling ground motion spectral matching","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01MozMYKDoaKrbyEpNoBPipD","content":[{"title":"In recent studies, spectral matching is the most commonly proposed method for selecting earthquake records for time-history analysis of structures. However, until now, there have been no serious investigations of the effects of coordinate values on the scaling of ground motions. This paper investigated the influence of using arithmetic and logarithmic values of response spectra in spectral matching procedures (i.e., ASM and LSM methods) on the results of nonlinear structural time-history analysis. Steel mom","url":"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/sv/2020/8180612/"},{"title":"Selection and spectral matching of recorded ground motions for seismic fragility analyses | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-022-01393-0"},{"title":"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear drift responses? | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227933785_Does_amplitude_scaling_of_ground_motion_records_result_in_biased_nonlinear_drift_responses"},{"title":"On selection and scaling of ground motions for analysis of seismically isolated structures | Earthquake Engineering and Engineering Vibration | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11803-016-0354-9"},{"title":"Introducing a response-based duration metric and its correlation with   structural damages","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10604"},{"title":"Amplitude scaling of ground motions as a potential source of bias: Large‐scale investigations on structural drifts - Tsalouchidis - 2022 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/eqe.3707"},{"title":"Seismic Time Histories","url":"https://www.structuremag.org/article/seismic-time-histories-a-practical-approach/"},{"title":"(PDF) Selection and Scaling Approaches of Earthquake Time‑Series for Structural Engineering Applications: A State‑of‑the‑Art Review","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/375888682_Selection_and_Scaling_Approaches_of_Earthquake_Time-Series_for_Structural_Engineering_Applications_A_State-of-the-Art_Review"},{"title":"NIST GCR 11-917-15 Selecting and Scaling Earthquake Ground","url":"https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/nistgcr11-917-15hires.pdf"}]},"I've completed the web search for your query. Here are the key findings related to scaled waveform blast seismic time history amplitude scaling, ground motion spectral matching:\n\n## Key Concepts Found:\n\n**Spectral Matching Procedures:**\nSpectral matching is the most commonly proposed method for selecting earthquake records for time-history analysis of structures.\n\n**Amplitude Scaling Methods:**\nWeighted scaling, also commonly referred to as amplitude scaling or linear scaling, as well as spectral matching, are investigated for ground motion selection. The amplitude of ground motions can be scaled to match the mean spectrum of selected ground motions and the target spectrum.\n\n**How Spectral Matching Works:**\nEach Fourier spectral ordinate is subjected to a different scaling factor, the amplitude of which depends on the distance between the original response spectrum and the target one. Larger scaling factors are applied to the high-frequency portion, mostly governed by small-scale effects poorly correlated with the earthquake source.\n\n**Applications:**\nGiven the rarity of Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCE) level events, it is common to scale specific acceleration records to match an MCE level event for a given site.\n\n**Bias Considerations:**\nScaled records can be safely used in nonlinear response-history analysis to assess the seismic structural behaviour if spectral and scenario compatibility are ensured and it is verified that the sustained amplitude is also consistent."],"durationSeconds":7.060070583000008,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01558evWyc4pz2jsmbtSy9UJ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast waveform simulation seed record scaling synthetic ground motion limitations waveform fidelity\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Simulation and generation of spectrum-compatible ground motions based on wavelet packet method - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726116300124\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic fragility curves for structures using non-parametric   representations\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.03876\"},{\"title\":\"Accelerating low-frequency ground motion simulation for finite fault sources using neural networks | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/234/3/2328/7202317\"},{\"title\":\"Full-waveform simulation of DAS records, response and cable-ground coupling | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/236/1/659/7424134\"},{\"title\":\"Broadband Ground Motion Synthesis via Generative Adversarial Neural   Operators: Development and Validation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.03447\"},{\"title\":\"Focal Mechanism Uncertainty Quantification In Ground Motion Simulations   Of Le Teil Earthquake\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.01868\"},{\"title\":\"Broadband Ground Motion Synthesis by Diffusion Model with Minimal Condition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.17333\"},{\"title\":\"Advancing data-driven broadband seismic wavefield simulation with multi-conditional diffusion model\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.14348v1\"},{\"title\":\"CyberShake Earthquake Fault Rupture Modeling and Ground Motion   Simulations for the Southwest Iceland Transform Zone\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.01533\"},{\"title\":\"Advancing data-driven broadband seismic wavefield simulation with   multi-conditional diffusion model\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.14348\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for your query on blast waveform simulation, seed record scaling, synthetic ground motion, and waveform fidelity limitations. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Key Research Findings\n\n**Waveform Fidelity Issues with Synthetic Ground Motions:**\nSynthetic ground motions usually have significant differences in waveform from recorded ground motions, which affects structural response in dynamic time history analysis.\n\n**Limitations of Scaled Records:**\nPeak ground acceleration (PGA) and Arias intensity levels of scaled and matched ground motions are difficult to maintain after spectral matching with existing time-domain methods. Additionally, scaling or modification of records that is not based on physics may only scale some intensities or frequency measures while leaving other elements unaltered, leading to unrealistic ground motions.\n\n**Advantages of Synthetic Ground Motions:**\nUsing synthetic ground motions overcomes limitations posed by the small number of records typically available for design scenarios and avoids the need to scale motions, allowing investigation of structural response for a large number of motions.\n\n**Frequency Band Limitations:**\nDue to limited frequency band of Green's functions, ground motion parameters can only be interpreted in the simulated frequency band.\n\n**Advanced Approaches for Waveform Generation:**\nRecent research has explored improved methods to enhance waveform fidelity, with some studies proposing machine learning approaches like diffusion models to produce high-fidelity synthetic data by progressively adding and reversing noise.\n\nThe search results emphasize the ongoing challenge of maintaining waveform realism and physical consistency when generating or scaling synthetic ground motions for seismic analysis.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"5369af1c-38be-42a2-ab75-b1354f92475b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:50.217Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast waveform simulation seed record scaling synthetic ground motion limitations waveform fidelity","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012XWq3sFr96RDRu2CgQYSDg","content":[{"title":"Simulation and generation of spectrum-compatible ground motions based on wavelet packet method - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726116300124"},{"title":"Seismic fragility curves for structures using non-parametric   representations","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.03876"},{"title":"Accelerating low-frequency ground motion simulation for finite fault sources using neural networks | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/234/3/2328/7202317"},{"title":"Full-waveform simulation of DAS records, response and cable-ground coupling | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/236/1/659/7424134"},{"title":"Broadband Ground Motion Synthesis via Generative Adversarial Neural   Operators: Development and Validation","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.03447"},{"title":"Focal Mechanism Uncertainty Quantification In Ground Motion Simulations   Of Le Teil Earthquake","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.01868"},{"title":"Broadband Ground Motion Synthesis by Diffusion Model with Minimal Condition","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.17333"},{"title":"Advancing data-driven broadband seismic wavefield simulation with multi-conditional diffusion model","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.14348v1"},{"title":"CyberShake Earthquake Fault Rupture Modeling and Ground Motion   Simulations for the Southwest Iceland Transform Zone","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.01533"},{"title":"Advancing data-driven broadband seismic wavefield simulation with   multi-conditional diffusion model","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.14348"}]},"I've performed the web search for your query on blast waveform simulation, seed record scaling, synthetic ground motion, and waveform fidelity limitations. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Key Research Findings\n\n**Waveform Fidelity Issues with Synthetic Ground Motions:**\nSynthetic ground motions usually have significant differences in waveform from recorded ground motions, which affects structural response in dynamic time history analysis.\n\n**Limitations of Scaled Records:**\nPeak ground acceleration (PGA) and Arias intensity levels of scaled and matched ground motions are difficult to maintain after spectral matching with existing time-domain methods. Additionally, scaling or modification of records that is not based on physics may only scale some intensities or frequency measures while leaving other elements unaltered, leading to unrealistic ground motions.\n\n**Advantages of Synthetic Ground Motions:**\nUsing synthetic ground motions overcomes limitations posed by the small number of records typically available for design scenarios and avoids the need to scale motions, allowing investigation of structural response for a large number of motions.\n\n**Frequency Band Limitations:**\nDue to limited frequency band of Green's functions, ground motion parameters can only be interpreted in the simulated frequency band.\n\n**Advanced Approaches for Waveform Generation:**\nRecent research has explored improved methods to enhance waveform fidelity, with some studies proposing machine learning approaches like diffusion models to produce high-fidelity synthetic data by progressively adding and reversing noise.\n\nThe search results emphasize the ongoing challenge of maintaining waveform realism and physical consistency when generating or scaling synthetic ground motions for seismic analysis."],"durationSeconds":9.116365875000003,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M1iTPoJ295SFL4TtfKz3z6","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"peak particle velocity\" \"component\" OR \"three-component\" blast prediction empirical equation site\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11680976/\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-81218-z\"},{\"title\":\"Particle peak velocity prediction based on risk-oriented hybrid ensemble learning | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-48622-z\"},{\"title\":\"Peak particle velocity prediction using support vector machines: A surface blasting case study\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532015000700012\"},{\"title\":\"Peak Particle Velocity Predicting Equation Associated with ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.scirp.org/pdf/jmmce2024126_32711099.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity and peak air pressure generated by buried explosion | Geotechnical and Geological Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00881024\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"},{\"title\":\"Predicting blast-induced peak particle velocity using BGAMs, ANN and SVM: a case study at the Nui Beo open-pit coal mine in Vietnam | Environmental Earth Sciences | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-019-8491-x\"}]\n\nI found search results related to peak particle velocity (PPV) prediction and empirical equations for blast prediction. Here's what I found:\n\n## Overview of PPV Prediction\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is one of the main indicators for determining the extent of ground vibration. There are three major methods cited in the literature for PPV prediction, including empirical, theoretical, and artificial intelligence techniques.\n\n## Empirical Equations\n\nTraditional empirical methods consider two main input parameters: maximum charge used per delay and distance between the blast face and the monitoring points. However, PPV is also affected by other factors, such as rock strength, rock mass discontinuities, and blast geometry, which have not been explicitly incorporated into these empirical equations.\n\n## Multi-Component Analysis\n\nNotably, research has modeled and analyzed blasting vibration from a multi-component perspective, demonstrating that reliance on a single vibration index cannot fully capture the complex response characteristics of blasting vibration.\n\n## Advanced Prediction Approaches\n\nThree sets of different formulae expressed as functions of dynamic elastic constants of rock and some design parameters involved in a buried explosion are derived explicitly for the prediction of peak particle velocity (PPV) and peak air pressure (PAP).\n\nWould you like more specific information about any particular empirical equation or prediction method?\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"67392fec-b0f9-461e-b2a7-9b9b5564f554","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:50:51.227Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"peak particle velocity\" \"component\" OR \"three-component\" blast prediction empirical equation site","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01MyCKpyQycAi5JD6fx4ezks","content":[{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11680976/"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-81218-z"},{"title":"Particle peak velocity prediction based on risk-oriented hybrid ensemble learning | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-48622-z"},{"title":"Peak particle velocity prediction using support vector machines: A surface blasting case study","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532015000700012"},{"title":"Peak Particle Velocity Predicting Equation Associated with ...","url":"https://www.scirp.org/pdf/jmmce2024126_32711099.pdf"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity and peak air pressure generated by buried explosion | Geotechnical and Geological Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00881024"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"},{"title":"Predicting blast-induced peak particle velocity using BGAMs, ANN and SVM: a case study at the Nui Beo open-pit coal mine in Vietnam | Environmental Earth Sciences | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-019-8491-x"}]},"I found search results related to peak particle velocity (PPV) prediction and empirical equations for blast prediction. Here's what I found:\n\n## Overview of PPV Prediction\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is one of the main indicators for determining the extent of ground vibration. There are three major methods cited in the literature for PPV prediction, including empirical, theoretical, and artificial intelligence techniques.\n\n## Empirical Equations\n\nTraditional empirical methods consider two main input parameters: maximum charge used per delay and distance between the blast face and the monitoring points. However, PPV is also affected by other factors, such as rock strength, rock mass discontinuities, and blast geometry, which have not been explicitly incorporated into these empirical equations.\n\n## Multi-Component Analysis\n\nNotably, research has modeled and analyzed blasting vibration from a multi-component perspective, demonstrating that reliance on a single vibration index cannot fully capture the complex response characteristics of blasting vibration.\n\n## Advanced Prediction Approaches\n\nThree sets of different formulae expressed as functions of dynamic elastic constants of rock and some design parameters involved in a buried explosion are derived explicitly for the prediction of peak particle velocity (PPV) and peak air pressure (PAP).\n\nWould you like more specific information about any particular empirical equation or prediction method?"],"durationSeconds":9.620898207999998,"searchCount":1}}
