{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GzqBd1rMRW152mPp1F7qzq","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration component PPA PPV scaling recorded waveform structural analysis seismic analogy\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Analysis and application of PPV vibration reduction technology in subgrade step blasting - a case study - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12098844/\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis of the Impact of ground vibration on the blast zone within\",\"url\":\"https://www.bio-conferences.org/articles/bioconf/pdf/2024/65/bioconf_btmic2024_01067.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis and application of PPV vibration reduction technology in subgrade step blasting - a case study | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-02269-4\"},{\"title\":\"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849\"},{\"title\":\"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888\"},{\"title\":\"Blasting Operations Using Different Initiation Methods in Deep   Underground Mines\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.02125\"},{\"title\":\"Vibration analysis for blasting\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9927340\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration components, PPV/PPA scaling, and recorded waveforms in structural analysis:\n\n## Vibration Components and Waveforms\n\nBlasting-induced vibration waves typically manifest as triaxial composite waveforms, with their attenuation characteristics governed by the spectral dominance of principal frequency components. The recorded vibrations are represented as waveforms, which consists of three wave components: transverse, vertical, and longitudinal (radial) orientations.\n\n## Measurement Methods\n\nExperimental measurements are conducted using an iSensor triaxial vibrometer, which provides high-precision recording of three-dimensional vibration velocity waveforms during blasting operations.\n\n## Peak Particle Metrics (PPV/PPA)\n\nVibrations in structures have typically been analysed based on measured motions of the particles in the structures, and subsequent determination of the peaks in velocity, acceleration or displacement of the particles, referred to as the peak particle velocity (PPV), the peak particle acceleration (PPA) or the peak particle displacement (PPD). Once determined, the PPV, PPA and/or PPD can be compared to preselected or predefined acceptable upper limits on peak velocity, acceleration or displacement to determine a likelihood of possible instability or damage in parts of the structure.\n\n## Structural Response and Linear Behavior\n\nThe masonry wall responds predominantly in a linear elastic regime, in which stresses are directly proportional to strains and are kinematically linked to local particle velocity.\n\n## Control Strategies\n\nActive control mainly realizes the regulation of PPV by optimizing the blasting parameters and improving the charge structure.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"b25b5241-c191-4522-99fe-fda29cd50ffd","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:51:06.316Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration component PPA PPV scaling recorded waveform structural analysis seismic analogy","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01QUmSvv8wqReV5CuX8EeP4G","content":[{"title":"Analysis and application of PPV vibration reduction technology in subgrade step blasting - a case study - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12098844/"},{"title":"Analysis of the Impact of ground vibration on the blast zone within","url":"https://www.bio-conferences.org/articles/bioconf/pdf/2024/65/bioconf_btmic2024_01067.pdf"},{"title":"Analysis and application of PPV vibration reduction technology in subgrade step blasting - a case study | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-02269-4"},{"title":"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849"},{"title":"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888"},{"title":"Blasting Operations Using Different Initiation Methods in Deep   Underground Mines","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.02125"},{"title":"Vibration analysis for blasting","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9927340"}]},"Based on the web search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration components, PPV/PPA scaling, and recorded waveforms in structural analysis:\n\n## Vibration Components and Waveforms\n\nBlasting-induced vibration waves typically manifest as triaxial composite waveforms, with their attenuation characteristics governed by the spectral dominance of principal frequency components. The recorded vibrations are represented as waveforms, which consists of three wave components: transverse, vertical, and longitudinal (radial) orientations.\n\n## Measurement Methods\n\nExperimental measurements are conducted using an iSensor triaxial vibrometer, which provides high-precision recording of three-dimensional vibration velocity waveforms during blasting operations.\n\n## Peak Particle Metrics (PPV/PPA)\n\nVibrations in structures have typically been analysed based on measured motions of the particles in the structures, and subsequent determination of the peaks in velocity, acceleration or displacement of the particles, referred to as the peak particle velocity (PPV), the peak particle acceleration (PPA) or the peak particle displacement (PPD). Once determined, the PPV, PPA and/or PPD can be compared to preselected or predefined acceptable upper limits on peak velocity, acceleration or displacement to determine a likelihood of possible instability or damage in parts of the structure.\n\n## Structural Response and Linear Behavior\n\nThe masonry wall responds predominantly in a linear elastic regime, in which stresses are directly proportional to strains and are kinematically linked to local particle velocity.\n\n## Control Strategies\n\nActive control mainly realizes the regulation of PPV by optimizing the blasting parameters and improving the charge structure."],"durationSeconds":6.706135832999993,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W8w5ZyaExGbnLGxVTr8N65","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration three orthogonal components independent correlated joint probability prediction surface charge distance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using multivariate\",\"url\":\"https://www.ias.ac.in/public/Volumes/sadh/049/00/0141.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting\"},{\"title\":\"Advancing the Prediction and Evaluation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Deep Ensemble Learning with Uncertainty Assessment\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/5/182\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Prediction of Blast Induced Vibration using Numerical Simulation\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318744047_Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Vibration_using_Numerical_Simulation\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/\"}]\n\nI found search results related to blast vibration prediction. Here's what the research shows:\n\n## Blast-Induced Ground Vibration in Three Orthogonal Directions\n\nBlast-induced ground vibrations in three orthogonal directions—transverse, vertical, and longitudinal—are recorded at different distances using seismographs. Longitudinal, transversal and vertical ground motion components are analyzed at monitoring points located at different distances from the blasting source.\n\n## Stochastic Nature and Prediction\n\nThe recordings belong to a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs, which could be distorted by a monotonic, instantaneous, time-independent nonlinear function. Neural network models with four main blast parameters as input—total charge, maximum charge per delay, distance from the blasting source to the measuring point, and hole depth—deliver significantly more accurate predictions.\n\n## Key Influence Factors\n\nThe distance from the blasting source has the strongest influence on the final value of the peak particle velocity. Critical factors influencing blast-induced vibrations include maximum section charge and horizontal distance.\n\nThe research indicates that blast vibration prediction models incorporate distance and charge weight as primary parameters, with the three orthogonal components being analyzed to characterize ground motion response.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"bb95cee9-7b5d-4537-9975-3c5d0da9e7a5","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:51:06.442Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration three orthogonal components independent correlated joint probability prediction surface charge distance","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01C29T3gCGr8apKBzxCVWqi2","content":[{"title":"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using multivariate","url":"https://www.ias.ac.in/public/Volumes/sadh/049/00/0141.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting"},{"title":"Advancing the Prediction and Evaluation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Deep Ensemble Learning with Uncertainty Assessment","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/5/182"},{"title":"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003"},{"title":"(PDF) Prediction of Blast Induced Vibration using Numerical Simulation","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318744047_Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Vibration_using_Numerical_Simulation"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/"}]},"I found search results related to blast vibration prediction. Here's what the research shows:\n\n## Blast-Induced Ground Vibration in Three Orthogonal Directions\n\nBlast-induced ground vibrations in three orthogonal directions—transverse, vertical, and longitudinal—are recorded at different distances using seismographs. Longitudinal, transversal and vertical ground motion components are analyzed at monitoring points located at different distances from the blasting source.\n\n## Stochastic Nature and Prediction\n\nThe recordings belong to a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs, which could be distorted by a monotonic, instantaneous, time-independent nonlinear function. Neural network models with four main blast parameters as input—total charge, maximum charge per delay, distance from the blasting source to the measuring point, and hole depth—deliver significantly more accurate predictions.\n\n## Key Influence Factors\n\nThe distance from the blasting source has the strongest influence on the final value of the peak particle velocity. Critical factors influencing blast-induced vibrations include maximum section charge and horizontal distance.\n\nThe research indicates that blast vibration prediction models incorporate distance and charge weight as primary parameters, with the three orthogonal components being analyzed to characterize ground motion response."],"durationSeconds":6.045825208000009,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DUW54rfwiMmCAP7Ukg6JmU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ISEE USBM RI 8507 blast vibration peak particle velocity three component measurement standard\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"RI 8507 Structure Response and Damage Produced by Ground Vibration From Surface Mine Blasting - Vibration Monitoring Course and Analysis Training\",\"url\":\"https://vibrationmonitoringcourse.com/osmre-ground-vibration-monitoring-papersosmre-office-surface-mining-reclamation-enforcement/ri-8507-structure-response-ground-vibration-mine-blasting/\"},{\"title\":\"OneMine | RI 8507 Structure Response and Damage Produced by Ground Vibration From Surface Mine Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.onemine.org/documents/ri-8507-structure-response-and-damage-produced-by-ground-vibration-from-surface-mine-blasting\"},{\"title\":\"The safe level blasting criteria from the USBM RI 8507 (Siskind et al.,... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/The-safe-level-blasting-criteria-from-the-USBM-RI-8507-Siskind-et-al-1980_fig1_290026411\"},{\"title\":\"Blast Vibration Basics Post #7 — Thoroughbred Drill and Blast Consultants\",\"url\":\"https://tbredblast.com/posts/blastvibration7\"},{\"title\":\"Drawbacks of Blast Vibration Regulations\",\"url\":\"https://vulcanhammer.net/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/blst-crt.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Deep Dive into ISEE/USBM Guidelines for Structural Safety\",\"url\":\"https://www.omnidots.com/en/blog/isee/usbm-guidelines-for-structural-safety\"},{\"title\":\"The GCs Guide to Construction Vibration Monitoring Equipment\",\"url\":\"https://www.eyrus.com/guide-to-construction-vibration-monitoring-equipment\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about ISEE USBM RI 8507 and its approach to blast vibration measurement:\n\n## Overview of RI 8507\n\nThe United States Bureau of Mines performed extensive vibration analysis and compiled the findings in the Report of Investigation 8507 (RI-8507): Structure Response and Damage Produced by Ground Vibration From Surface Mine Blasting in 1989, which studied 76 structures that experienced 219 production blasts and also reviewed data from previous studies.\n\n## Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) Measurement\n\nParticle velocity is still the best single ground motion descriptor. Importantly, most widely used guidelines (DIN 4150-3, USBM, ISEE, etc.) assume you're measuring Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) on all three axes and then using the highest of the three to check against limits.\n\n## Safe Level Standards\n\nSafe levels of ground vibration from blasting range from 0.5 to 2.0 in/sec peak particle velocity for residential-type structures.\n\n## Connection to ISEE\n\nThe United States Bureau of Mines Report of Investigations 8507 (USBM RI-8507) is one commonly referenced source, along with subsequent revisions and clarifications by the Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement (OSMRE) and International Society of Explosives Engineers (ISEE).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"c7d167e9-e972-4f9c-ae27-12c312d90954","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:51:07.039Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ISEE USBM RI 8507 blast vibration peak particle velocity three component measurement standard","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01PBj3L5tMJbM5VaG2qDi8W6","content":[{"title":"RI 8507 Structure Response and Damage Produced by Ground Vibration From Surface Mine Blasting - Vibration Monitoring Course and Analysis Training","url":"https://vibrationmonitoringcourse.com/osmre-ground-vibration-monitoring-papersosmre-office-surface-mining-reclamation-enforcement/ri-8507-structure-response-ground-vibration-mine-blasting/"},{"title":"OneMine | RI 8507 Structure Response and Damage Produced by Ground Vibration From Surface Mine Blasting","url":"https://www.onemine.org/documents/ri-8507-structure-response-and-damage-produced-by-ground-vibration-from-surface-mine-blasting"},{"title":"The safe level blasting criteria from the USBM RI 8507 (Siskind et al.,... | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/The-safe-level-blasting-criteria-from-the-USBM-RI-8507-Siskind-et-al-1980_fig1_290026411"},{"title":"Blast Vibration Basics Post #7 — Thoroughbred Drill and Blast Consultants","url":"https://tbredblast.com/posts/blastvibration7"},{"title":"Drawbacks of Blast Vibration Regulations","url":"https://vulcanhammer.net/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/blst-crt.pdf"},{"title":"A Deep Dive into ISEE/USBM Guidelines for Structural Safety","url":"https://www.omnidots.com/en/blog/isee/usbm-guidelines-for-structural-safety"},{"title":"The GCs Guide to Construction Vibration Monitoring Equipment","url":"https://www.eyrus.com/guide-to-construction-vibration-monitoring-equipment"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about ISEE USBM RI 8507 and its approach to blast vibration measurement:\n\n## Overview of RI 8507\n\nThe United States Bureau of Mines performed extensive vibration analysis and compiled the findings in the Report of Investigation 8507 (RI-8507): Structure Response and Damage Produced by Ground Vibration From Surface Mine Blasting in 1989, which studied 76 structures that experienced 219 production blasts and also reviewed data from previous studies.\n\n## Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) Measurement\n\nParticle velocity is still the best single ground motion descriptor. Importantly, most widely used guidelines (DIN 4150-3, USBM, ISEE, etc.) assume you're measuring Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) on all three axes and then using the highest of the three to check against limits.\n\n## Safe Level Standards\n\nSafe levels of ground vibration from blasting range from 0.5 to 2.0 in/sec peak particle velocity for residential-type structures.\n\n## Connection to ISEE\n\nThe United States Bureau of Mines Report of Investigations 8507 (USBM RI-8507) is one commonly referenced source, along with subsequent revisions and clarifications by the Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement (OSMRE) and International Society of Explosives Engineers (ISEE)."],"durationSeconds":5.3424400000000025,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FgfH95FoC1TrxceMubDx1U","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast source model physics-based waveform versus empirical scaled distance limitations frequency content\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8548544/\"},{\"title\":\"IOP Conference Series: Materials Science and Engineering PAPER • OPEN ACCESS\",\"url\":\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1757-899X/1038/1/012083/pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic waves generated by explosions in, and above, saturated sediments: the Foulness seismoacoustic coupling trials | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/242/3/ggaf178/8203305\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"A Deep Surrogate Model for Robust and Generalizable Long-Term Blast Wave Prediction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.18168v1\"},{\"title\":\"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast source models comparing physics-based waveforms versus empirical scaled distance approaches:\n\n## Empirical Scaled Distance Models\n\nEmpirical formula approaches based on scaled distance theory have dominated blast vibration prediction methods since the environmental movement of the 1960s-1970s. The scaled distance (SD) is calculated as SD = R/Q^(1/2), where R is the distance between the source and target, and Q is the explosive amount per delay.\n\n## Limitations of Empirical Approaches\n\nFrequency-dependent attenuation and geometric dispersion effects during stress wave propagation cause vibration responses to exhibit pronounced nonlinear characteristics, posing significant challenges to traditional prediction methods based on linear assumptions. Additionally, empirical equations show agreement with experimental and hydrocode results for scaled distance values greater than 1 m·kg^(-1/3), indicating there are distance-related constraints on their applicability.\n\n## Physics-Based Waveform Approaches\n\nVibration models using multiple seed waveforms have been applied to predict particle velocity and frequency content, representing a more physics-based approach. Research shows that amplitude variations with distance are controlled by geometrical propagation effects (such as multipathing through layered structures) rather than solely by explosive source effects.\n\n## Frequency Content Considerations\n\nHigh-frequency components above 150 Hz decay fastest, while as propagation distance increases, low-frequency components of the waveform at 0–100 Hz increase proportionally and dominate, showing that frequency response varies significantly with distance in blast propagation.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"a2f57bd7-46d8-4190-aff4-8658b46f5d0a","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:51:08.906Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast source model physics-based waveform versus empirical scaled distance limitations frequency content","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01VgsfQcEqswYaec8cAq5ZbQ","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8548544/"},{"title":"IOP Conference Series: Materials Science and Engineering PAPER • OPEN ACCESS","url":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1757-899X/1038/1/012083/pdf"},{"title":"Seismic waves generated by explosions in, and above, saturated sediments: the Foulness seismoacoustic coupling trials | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/242/3/ggaf178/8203305"},{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"A Deep Surrogate Model for Robust and Generalizable Long-Term Blast Wave Prediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.18168v1"},{"title":"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888"},{"title":"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast source models comparing physics-based waveforms versus empirical scaled distance approaches:\n\n## Empirical Scaled Distance Models\n\nEmpirical formula approaches based on scaled distance theory have dominated blast vibration prediction methods since the environmental movement of the 1960s-1970s. The scaled distance (SD) is calculated as SD = R/Q^(1/2), where R is the distance between the source and target, and Q is the explosive amount per delay.\n\n## Limitations of Empirical Approaches\n\nFrequency-dependent attenuation and geometric dispersion effects during stress wave propagation cause vibration responses to exhibit pronounced nonlinear characteristics, posing significant challenges to traditional prediction methods based on linear assumptions. Additionally, empirical equations show agreement with experimental and hydrocode results for scaled distance values greater than 1 m·kg^(-1/3), indicating there are distance-related constraints on their applicability.\n\n## Physics-Based Waveform Approaches\n\nVibration models using multiple seed waveforms have been applied to predict particle velocity and frequency content, representing a more physics-based approach. Research shows that amplitude variations with distance are controlled by geometrical propagation effects (such as multipathing through layered structures) rather than solely by explosive source effects.\n\n## Frequency Content Considerations\n\nHigh-frequency components above 150 Hz decay fastest, while as propagation distance increases, low-frequency components of the waveform at 0–100 Hz increase proportionally and dominate, showing that frequency response varies significantly with distance in blast propagation."],"durationSeconds":6.7540977080000015,"searchCount":1}}
