{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EidRvgqgNeqA3dYFyrgf3s","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"stochastic blast vibration component prediction covariance Ortega 2013 PLOS ONE independent Gaussian\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.matjazperc.com/publications/PLoSONE_8_e82056.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Development of a blast-induced vibration prediction model using an artificial neural network\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532019000200013\"},{\"title\":\"Stochastic response analysis for nonlinear vibration systems with adjustable stiffness property under random excitation | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0200922\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/\"}]\n\nI found the paper you're searching for! The 2013 PLOS ONE article \"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\" was authored by Kostić, Perc, Vasović, and Trajković.\n\nThe study analyzed longitudinal, transversal and vertical ground motion components at three monitoring points and found that the recordings belong to a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs, which could be distorted by a monotonic, instantaneous, time-independent nonlinear function. The authors proposed an improved prediction model for peak particle velocity based on a neural network.\n\nThe paper is available at: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\n\n(Note: The search results show authors named Kostić, Perc, Vasović, and Trajković rather than \"Ortega\" as mentioned in your query.)\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"70a378ad-2213-43e4-bbef-9a91f512b1e3","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:51:24.214Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"stochastic blast vibration component prediction covariance Ortega 2013 PLOS ONE independent Gaussian","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0117i23qRYasD9oaHBFNhRun","content":[{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground ...","url":"https://www.matjazperc.com/publications/PLoSONE_8_e82056.pdf"},{"title":"Development of a blast-induced vibration prediction model using an artificial neural network","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532019000200013"},{"title":"Stochastic response analysis for nonlinear vibration systems with adjustable stiffness property under random excitation | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0200922"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/"}]},"I found the paper you're searching for! The 2013 PLOS ONE article \"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\" was authored by Kostić, Perc, Vasović, and Trajković.\n\nThe study analyzed longitudinal, transversal and vertical ground motion components at three monitoring points and found that the recordings belong to a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs, which could be distorted by a monotonic, instantaneous, time-independent nonlinear function. The authors proposed an improved prediction model for peak particle velocity based on a neural network.\n\nThe paper is available at: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\n\n(Note: The search results show authors named Kostić, Perc, Vasović, and Trajković rather than \"Ortega\" as mentioned in your query.)"],"durationSeconds":3.858068583,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018QagR22gHZTQpnEJD2Axvx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration spectral matching versus amplitude scaling seismic hazard analogy differences\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Engineering Safety-Oriented Blasting-Induced Seismic Wave Signal Processing: An EMD Endpoint Suppression Method Based on Multi-Scale Feature - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12252125/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Selection and Scaling Approaches of Earthquake Time‑Series for Structural Engineering Applications: A State‑of‑the‑Art Review\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/375888682_Selection_and_Scaling_Approaches_of_Earthquake_Time-Series_for_Structural_Engineering_Applications_A_State-of-the-Art_Review\"},{\"title\":\"Spectral Matching in Time Domain: A Seismological and Engineering Analysis | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325308389_Spectral_Matching_in_Time_Domain_A_Seismological_and_Engineering_Analysis\"},{\"title\":\"Alternative Approaches to Vibration Measurement Due to the Blasting Operation: A Pilot Study - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6806356/\"},{\"title\":\"Engineering Safety-Oriented Blasting-Induced Seismic Wave Signal Processing: An EMD Endpoint Suppression Method Based on Multi-Scale Feature\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12252125/\"},{\"title\":\"On selection and scaling of ground motions for analysis of seismically isolated structures | Earthquake Engineering and Engineering Vibration | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11803-016-0354-9\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Composition and Characteristics of Rock Vibration Generated in Blasting Excavation of Deep Tunnels\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.903773/full\"},{\"title\":\"Vibration prediction with a method based on the absorption property of blast-induced seismic waves: A case study\",\"url\":\"https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/geo-2022-0633/html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information comparing spectral matching and amplitude scaling approaches in seismic hazard analysis:\n\n## Key Differences Between Spectral Matching and Amplitude Scaling\n\n**Amplitude Scaling Approach:**\nAmplitude scaling only scales the amplitude of the seed motions and does not change its frequency content. Amplitude (up-)scaling tends to make records more aggressive than real, un-scaled records with spectra that are naturally at that level.\n\n**Spectral Matching Approach:**\nSpectral matching involves modifying the frequency content and phasing of real records to match a smooth target spectrum.\n\n**Comparative Performance:**\nSpectrum matching is generally more stable than scaling both in terms of the bias as well as the resulting dispersion in the predicted demands. This suggests that spectral matching provides more consistent and reliable results for seismic hazard analysis compared to simple amplitude scaling.\n\nThe fundamental difference is that amplitude scaling preserves the original frequency characteristics of the seismic waves while only adjusting their magnitude, whereas spectral matching modifies both the amplitude and frequency content to match target seismic hazard specifications—making it a more comprehensive approach but potentially more computationally intensive.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"2bde0340-b121-4225-8c8b-1538bf7ad1e7","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:51:27.159Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration spectral matching versus amplitude scaling seismic hazard analogy differences","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014ihCVd1EDRCJG3fJTpHNbd","content":[{"title":"Engineering Safety-Oriented Blasting-Induced Seismic Wave Signal Processing: An EMD Endpoint Suppression Method Based on Multi-Scale Feature - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12252125/"},{"title":"(PDF) Selection and Scaling Approaches of Earthquake Time‑Series for Structural Engineering Applications: A State‑of‑the‑Art Review","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/375888682_Selection_and_Scaling_Approaches_of_Earthquake_Time-Series_for_Structural_Engineering_Applications_A_State-of-the-Art_Review"},{"title":"Spectral Matching in Time Domain: A Seismological and Engineering Analysis | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325308389_Spectral_Matching_in_Time_Domain_A_Seismological_and_Engineering_Analysis"},{"title":"Alternative Approaches to Vibration Measurement Due to the Blasting Operation: A Pilot Study - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6806356/"},{"title":"Engineering Safety-Oriented Blasting-Induced Seismic Wave Signal Processing: An EMD Endpoint Suppression Method Based on Multi-Scale Feature","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12252125/"},{"title":"On selection and scaling of ground motions for analysis of seismically isolated structures | Earthquake Engineering and Engineering Vibration | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11803-016-0354-9"},{"title":"Frontiers | Composition and Characteristics of Rock Vibration Generated in Blasting Excavation of Deep Tunnels","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.903773/full"},{"title":"Vibration prediction with a method based on the absorption property of blast-induced seismic waves: A case study","url":"https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/geo-2022-0633/html"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information comparing spectral matching and amplitude scaling approaches in seismic hazard analysis:\n\n## Key Differences Between Spectral Matching and Amplitude Scaling\n\n**Amplitude Scaling Approach:**\nAmplitude scaling only scales the amplitude of the seed motions and does not change its frequency content. Amplitude (up-)scaling tends to make records more aggressive than real, un-scaled records with spectra that are naturally at that level.\n\n**Spectral Matching Approach:**\nSpectral matching involves modifying the frequency content and phasing of real records to match a smooth target spectrum.\n\n**Comparative Performance:**\nSpectrum matching is generally more stable than scaling both in terms of the bias as well as the resulting dispersion in the predicted demands. This suggests that spectral matching provides more consistent and reliable results for seismic hazard analysis compared to simple amplitude scaling.\n\nThe fundamental difference is that amplitude scaling preserves the original frequency characteristics of the seismic waves while only adjusting their magnitude, whereas spectral matching modifies both the amplitude and frequency content to match target seismic hazard specifications—making it a more comprehensive approach but potentially more computationally intensive."],"durationSeconds":5.323099042000002,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BG71iXj2DW4CDATYjHQSMA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"seed waveform blast vibration scaling technique Dowding Blair charge weight amplitude match\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Charge Weight Scaling Laws and the Superposition of Blast Vibration Waves\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232922308_Charge_Weight_Scaling_Laws_and_the_Superposition_of_Blast_Vibration_Waves\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Modeling the Effect of Delay Scatter on Peak Particle Velocity of Blast Vibration Using a Multiple Seed Waveform Vibration Model\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/311923105_Modeling_the_Effect_of_Delay_Scatter_on_Peak_Particle_Velocity_of_Blast_Vibration_Using_a_Multiple_Seed_Waveform_Vibration_Model\"},{\"title\":\"Frequency channeling: a concept to increase the frequency and control the PPV of blast-induced ground vibration waves in multi-hole blast in a surface mine | Bulletin of Engineering Geology and the Environment | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10064-021-02400-5\"},{\"title\":\"Non-linear superposition models of blast vibration - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160907000792\"},{\"title\":\"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160913001822\"},{\"title\":\"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/259522141_Blast_vibration_dependence_on_charge_length_velocity_of_detonation_and_layered_media\"},{\"title\":\"Experimental and theoretical analysis of charge length on single-hole vibration amplitude from underground deep-hole blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160924002417\"},{\"title\":\"Modelling of blast vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0148906288909849\"},{\"title\":\"Typical blast vibration velocity waveform computed by the seed... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Typical-blast-vibration-velocity-waveform-computed-by-the-seed-wave-based-superposition_fig9_337784612\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about the seed waveform blast vibration scaling technique used by Dowding and Blair:\n\n## Overview\n\nWaveform superposition techniques, also known as seed waveform or signature hole methods, are used to predict the ground vibrations generated by a production blast.\n\n## Charge Weight Scaling and Amplitude Matching\n\nThe amplitude attenuation is modeled according to the relation between the peak particle velocity (PPV) and the charge weight scaled distance established from the signature hole vibration monitoring. The use of multiple sets of signature waveforms measured at different distances takes into account the effect of p-, sand nd surface waves and the geology at a site.\n\n## Superposition Method\n\nCurrent waveform models of blast vibration typically consider a linear superposition of characteristic (seed) waveforms. However, Current waveform models of blast vibration typically consider a linear superposition of characteristic (seed) waveforms. However, blasting produces large strains in the surrounding medium, which, in turn, implies a non-linear response of the material. It is therefore questionable to use a linear superposition scheme to add the contributions of individual charge masses.\n\n## Application and Results\n\nUsing the site parameters from the analysis of signature hole vibrations, the present blast vibration model yields reasonable agreement with field measurements in terms of vibration amplitude and frequency.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e","uuid":"8271672d-21c7-4659-8645-0e39271fd140","timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:51:27.520Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"seed waveform blast vibration scaling technique Dowding Blair charge weight amplitude match","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01TABvJUsqgnkVReWy3WHwR9","content":[{"title":"Charge Weight Scaling Laws and the Superposition of Blast Vibration Waves","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232922308_Charge_Weight_Scaling_Laws_and_the_Superposition_of_Blast_Vibration_Waves"},{"title":"(PDF) Modeling the Effect of Delay Scatter on Peak Particle Velocity of Blast Vibration Using a Multiple Seed Waveform Vibration Model","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/311923105_Modeling_the_Effect_of_Delay_Scatter_on_Peak_Particle_Velocity_of_Blast_Vibration_Using_a_Multiple_Seed_Waveform_Vibration_Model"},{"title":"Frequency channeling: a concept to increase the frequency and control the PPV of blast-induced ground vibration waves in multi-hole blast in a surface mine | Bulletin of Engineering Geology and the Environment | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10064-021-02400-5"},{"title":"Non-linear superposition models of blast vibration - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160907000792"},{"title":"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160913001822"},{"title":"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/259522141_Blast_vibration_dependence_on_charge_length_velocity_of_detonation_and_layered_media"},{"title":"Experimental and theoretical analysis of charge length on single-hole vibration amplitude from underground deep-hole blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160924002417"},{"title":"Modelling of blast vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0148906288909849"},{"title":"Typical blast vibration velocity waveform computed by the seed... | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Typical-blast-vibration-velocity-waveform-computed-by-the-seed-wave-based-superposition_fig9_337784612"}]},"Based on the search results, here's information about the seed waveform blast vibration scaling technique used by Dowding and Blair:\n\n## Overview\n\nWaveform superposition techniques, also known as seed waveform or signature hole methods, are used to predict the ground vibrations generated by a production blast.\n\n## Charge Weight Scaling and Amplitude Matching\n\nThe amplitude attenuation is modeled according to the relation between the peak particle velocity (PPV) and the charge weight scaled distance established from the signature hole vibration monitoring. The use of multiple sets of signature waveforms measured at different distances takes into account the effect of p-, sand nd surface waves and the geology at a site.\n\n## Superposition Method\n\nCurrent waveform models of blast vibration typically consider a linear superposition of characteristic (seed) waveforms. However, Current waveform models of blast vibration typically consider a linear superposition of characteristic (seed) waveforms. However, blasting produces large strains in the surrounding medium, which, in turn, implies a non-linear response of the material. It is therefore questionable to use a linear superposition scheme to add the contributions of individual charge masses.\n\n## Application and Results\n\nUsing the site parameters from the analysis of signature hole vibrations, the present blast vibration model yields reasonable agreement with field measurements in terms of vibration amplitude and frequency."],"durationSeconds":6.060300124999994,"searchCount":1}}
