{"type":"system","subtype":"thinking_tokens","estimated_tokens":18141,"estimated_tokens_delta":11049,"uuid":"6c8f8e94-28e6-490d-95f3-09796c8f22ce","session_id":"4570178e-7a67-41c2-96b2-15672ad3315e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_018rv7GtvfXEBy3sqTD76B2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough information to write the full research manuscript. Let me compile what I've learned across all 5 slots.\n\n**Slot 1: Treatment of component PPV/PPA responses**\n- Blast vibration is typically recorded as three orthogonal components: transverse (T), vertical (V), and longitudinal (radial) (L)\n- Most empirical literature uses the maximum of the three components (maximum component peak) or the resultant (vector sum)\n- Some studies do analyze all three components separately\n- Standards (USBM RI-8507, ISEE) typically use the maximum component PPV for compliance\n- There are three main ways to calculate PPV: the maximum component peak, the true resultant computed at a simultaneous time, or the maximum vector sum using independent peaks—and this distinction between component-wise and resultant approaches is a critical methodological issue\n\n**Slot 2: Methods for multivariate or constrained response prediction**\n- I'm considering several approaches to handle the correlations between the three vibration components: principal component analysis combined with multivariate logarithmic regression, Gaussian process regression to explicitly model variable correlations, and joint probability methods\n- There's also the challenge of multicollinearity when predicting multiple correlated components, which suggests exploring constrained optimization where one component cannot exceed the resultant\n\n**Slot 3: Literature precedent for component-peak scaling**\n- The seed waveform technique in blast vibration literature scales recorded time histories by the ratio of predicted PPV to recorded PPV, which is the closest precedent for what I'm trying to do\n- However, this approach is typically applied to the vector sum rather than individual components, so component-specific scaling is less established in the literature\n\n**Slot 4: Distinguishing scenario scaling from related methods**\n- I need to clarify how scenario scaling differs from amplitude scaling (which preserves frequency content and temporal structure), spectral matching (which modifies frequency content to match a target spectrum), and synthetic blast simulation (which physically generates waveforms from source parameters)\n- Scenario scaling uses a recorded blast as a template and scales each component's amplitude by the ratio of predicted to recorded peak values\n\n**Slot 5: Key limitations of this approach**\n- The method doesn't account for how frequency content changes with distance, since high-frequency energy decays faster than low-frequency energy\n- Phase relationships between components aren't physically grounded, and I'm not capturing source-site path effects, ground condition variations, or how duration and significant cycle counts should scale with the scenario\n\nNow I'm ready to structure and write out the full manuscript following the five slots. I'm clarifying the formatting rules based on the workflow instructions—the task specifies \"flowing paragraphs\" for each slot, which means no bullet points within those sections, and I need to verify whether subheadings are allowed at the slot level itself. I'm laying out the technical distinctions between how blast vibration is measured—component peak (the maximum value on any single axis), true resultant (the vector magnitude at its peak moment), and maximum vector sum (combining peaks from different times). Most literature models ground motion as a single scalar PPV value, which is what I need to address in this section.\n\nFor the multivariate approach, I'm considering dimension reduction techniques like PCA followed by logarithmic regression, along with Gaussian process regression, while grappling with the challenge that all three components from the same blast event are inherently correlated—something few papers explicitly account for in their modeling.\n\nThe seed waveform technique from prior work scales recorded blast signals by amplitude factors based on predicted versus observed PPV ratios, but it typically applies uniform scaling across the entire waveform rather than adjusting each component peak independently, which is where my approach differs.\n\nNow I'm contrasting scenario scaling against other waveform modification methods: spectral matching adjusts frequency content to match a target spectrum, while synthetic simulation generates waveforms from physics-based source parameters. Scenario scaling, by contrast, preserves the original frequency and phase structure and applies only a scalar multiplier per component based on the peak ratio—but this simplicity introduces several limitations I need to address. The frequency-distance relationship means high frequencies decay faster than low frequencies, so a simple amplitude scale doesn't capture that attenuation. The phase relationships between components aren't physically grounded to the scenario, and the duration, temporal envelope, and non-stationarity characteristics all remain unadjusted.\n\nI'm now drafting the manuscript with proper citations and audit footnotes, being careful to cite only the sources I actually use. I'm also looking at research on charge weight scaling laws and how blast vibrations superpose, along with studies on delay scatter effects and waveform analysis techniques for vibration mitigation. I need to stay focused on the component-wise and multivariate modeling gap that the scope requires, rather than getting sidetracked by generic attenuation overviews. Now I'm structuring the manuscript to address how three-axis vibration data is typically recorded but most prediction models collapse this into a single scalar PPV value, missing the opportunity to model each component's attenuation separately—and I'm noting that PPA modeling is even less standardized than PPV in the literature.\n\nThe core gap I'm identifying is that while components are inherently correlated because they originate from the same blast-path system, the field treats them independently rather than jointly. I'm also flagging that constrained prediction—ensuring the resultant remains consistent with individual component predictions—is rarely addressed explicitly in the literature, and I'm preparing to discuss how charge-weight scaling laws and signature-hole techniques could inform component-specific modeling approaches. The frequency content and duration characteristics are locked to the source scenario of the template blast, missing the high-frequency decay that would occur at greater distances, and the independently-scaled components lack physical grounding in inter-component phase relationships. I'm now drafting the full manuscript with careful attention to only citing sources I've actually retrieved and making claims supported by those sources. a triaxial phenomenon measured simultaneously on vertical, longitudinal, and transverse axes, with peak particle velocity and acceleration extracted from each component's waveform. The standard approach in regulatory frameworks like USBM RI-8507 and ISEE guidance uses the maximum of the three component peaks as the controlling metric for compliance, rather than computing a true resultant velocity. [WEB:https://vibrationmonitoringcourse.com/osmre-ground-vibration-monitoring-papersosmre-office-surface-mining-reclamation-enforcement/ri-8507-structure-response-ground-vibration-mine-blasting/]\n\nThere's an important distinction between how peak particle velocity can be defined. The maximum component peak is simply the largest absolute value recorded on any single axis, while the true resultant is the vector magnitude at the moment when the three-component velocity vector itself reaches its maximum. A third approach, the maximum vector sum, takes the square root of the sum of squares of the individual component peaks regardless of timing, which introduces an overly conservative safety factor and is now discouraged in practice. [WEB:https://www.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:1025\n\n939/FULLTEXT01.pdf]\n\nThe mathematical relationship shows that the true resultant particle velocity differs from the maximum vector sum because the peaks on each axis occur at different times, so you can't simply combine the individual maximum values without accounting for this temporal offset.\n\nMost empirical blast vibration models use a simplified scalar approach, treating peak particle velocity as a function of scaled distance—the ratio of slant distance to charge weight raised to a power (typically 2 for surface blasts or 3 for underground work). The standard form follows a power law where PPV decreases with scaled distance, with site-specific constants determined from field data.\n\nIn practice, researchers typically collapse the three directional components (longitudinal, transversal, vertical) into a single dataset by selecting the maximum component value at each station, effectively assuming the components are interchangeable. However, some studies in the literature—including work by Kostic and colleagues published in PLOS ONE—take a different approach by analyzing each component separately to generate component-specific predictions.\n\nOther research has developed parallel empirical equations for both the scalar peak particle velocity (maximum component at a station) and a combined measure treating all three components as a single population, though this doesn't yield separate attenuation surfaces for each axis. Additionally, there's evidence that attenuation parameters vary systematically depending on wave type: P-waves dominate the longitudinal component, S-waves the transverse, and Rayleigh surface waves the vertical at lower frequencies.\n\nThe different wave types decay at different rates, so pooling all three component peaks as equivalent samples from a single attenuation model introduces noise that's actually rooted in the physical composition of the waves themselves. Component-level analyses remain less common than scalar approaches in the literature. Moving into peak particle acceleration modeling, which follows a similar but less mature research trajectory.\n\nPPA can be obtained either by differentiating velocity records numerically or directly from accelerometer data, and it follows the same power-law scaling with distance as PPV. While vibration monitoring treats PPV, PPA, and peak particle displacement as interchangeable representations linked through dominant frequency, published attenuation models for component-level PPA are far fewer than those for PPV. Most regulatory frameworks specify PPV thresholds rather than PPA, which has historically steered research focus toward velocity attenuation over acceleration component surfaces.\n\nWhen blast vibration components are treated as correlated outputs from the same source-propagation system, the prediction problem becomes multivariate—requiring characterization of the joint distribution of the three component peaks or simultaneous prediction of multiple responses while accounting for their dependence structure. The literature employs various approaches ranging from conventional multivariate regression to dimension-reduction techniques and probabilistic process models.\n\nMultivariate statistical regression directly applies to blast vibration data by formulating a system of prediction equations where blast design variables like maximum instantaneous charge, distance, hole depth, burden, spacing, and initiation timing serve as shared predictors across multiple ground motion metrics. A key challenge in this approach is multicollinearity among the blast design predictors, which inflates variance in the coefficient estimates and undermines the reliability of model inferences.\n\nPrincipal component analysis has been used as a preprocessing step before multivariate logarithmic regression, creating orthogonal linear combinations of the correlated blast design predictors to capture maximum variance, with regression then performed on these uncorrelated principal component scores rather than the original predictors. process parameterised by source and path variables. Kostic and colleagues showed that blast-induced vibration time histories in three orthogonal directions can be modelled as stationary Gaussian linear stochastic processes, where inter-component dependence stems from shared-source excitation. This framework suggests the joint distribution of component peaks depends on marginal distributions and inter-component covariance, enabling conditional prediction through Gaussian relations, though the cited work characterised observed time histories rather than explicitly predicting component peaks across a charge-distance surface, leaving a gap between the modelling framework and practical attenuation surface construction.\n\nGaussian process regression offers another approach for ground vibration prediction, providing a non-parametric probabilistic framework with uncertainty quantification through kernel-based covariance modelling. Multi-output GPR formulations can jointly model correlated component peaks, though research highlights that multicollinearity among predictors and careful covariance function specification are critical for reliable results.\n\nMulti-output GPR with cross-covariance kernels is a well-established statistical framework capable of representing inter-component correlations, but its specific application to joint prediction of three-component blast vibration peaks across a charge-distance surface hasn't been extensively demonstrated in the blast vibration literature. Constrained prediction approaches, where predicted component peaks must satisfy physical consistency criteria—such as ensuring the maximum component doesn't exceed the vector resultant—represent another avenue worth exploring.\n\nThe maximum vector sum concept is acknowledged in the literature as a conservative bound that combines independently measured component peaks without considering simultaneity, but this doesn't address how attenuation models should enforce joint consistency of predicted component responses. This gap between the implicit recognition of these constraints and the lack of explicit methods to enforce them during prediction represents a genuine operational need for constructing component-wise prediction surfaces that are physically coherent.\n\nThe seed waveform methodology—also called the signature hole or single-hole technique—addresses multi-hole blast prediction by taking a measured reference waveform from a single representative charge at the monitoring location, then scaling and time-shifting that waveform to represent each delayed detonation's contribution, with all contributions summed through linear superposition to predict the full production blast response. The amplitude scaling factor in this approach is determined by... the scaling factor comes from a single regression fitted to the reference waveform's amplitude rather than separate equations for each component, so the seed waveform method applies one uniform scalar across all three axes without targeting independent peak values for each direction. This means component-specific scaling isn't part of the established methodology as documented in the literature, though I'm noting medium confidence here since full access to all formulations wasn't available. Beyond this technique, the broader blast vibration field doesn't seem to have a well-established practice of taking a recorded blast event and rescaling each of its three component time histories independently to match separately predicted component peak targets—which contrasts with earthquake engineering, where this kind of component-wise amplitude scaling to match target intensity measures is thoroughly documented and mature. Looking through the published blast vibration research, I haven't found a study where recorded blast waveforms were scaled separately on each axis to match independently predicted component peak velocities or accelerations derived from component-specific attenuation surfaces.\n\nFull waveform prediction using deep learning and Volterra series models generates synthetic time histories conditioned on source and path parameters without needing explicit component-specific scaling, but these approaches focus on overall PPV and dominant frequency rather than matching component-specific peak values against independently derived attenuation predictions—a meaningful distinction between waveform simulation and peak-matched scaling.\n\nNow I'm defining scenario scaling more precisely: it's a procedure where a recorded triaxial blast waveform serves as a template, with each component multiplied by a scalar factor to match target values derived from empirical attenuation predictions, such that the predicted transverse, vertical, and longitudinal PPV at a given charge-distance combination align with the scaled record peaks. Spectral matching works differently—it adjusts the frequency content by applying frequency-dependent scaling factors to match a target response spectrum, which can amplify or attenuate different frequency bands independently and alter the amplitude envelope and spectral shape. Scenario scaling, by contrast, applies a single uniform scalar across all time steps on each axis, leaving the original frequency content and relative phasing intact. These are fundamentally distinct operations with different physical implications.\n\nTarget-spectrum adjustment is the broader framework that encompasses both selecting records compatible with a target spectrum derived from probabilistic seismic hazard analysis and spectrally matching records to that target.\n\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of target-spectrum adjustment in earthquake engineering is confirmed by the NIST report. The contrast with scenario scaling is analytically derived from the distinction between spectral and peak-based representations. Confidence is HIGH for the distinction, with the caveat that this characterisation is largely derived from earthquake engineering rather than blast vibration sources.] In blast vibration contexts, the prediction surface is an empirical attenuation equation for a single peak intensity measure rather than a probabilistic spectral hazard representation, so scenario scaling to component peaks lacks both the probabilistic hazard foundation and the spectral energy redistribution that characterizes target-spectrum adjustment.\n\nNow I'm moving into synthetic blast waveform simulation, which encompasses methods that generate time histories from first principles or parameterized models.\n\n^[WEB:https://www.researchgate.net/publication/284509528_Near-field_blast_vibration_models] Physics-based approaches model the explosive source as a pressurized cavity in an elastic medium, propagate stress waves through numerical methods or Green's functions, and recover particle motion at the receiver. Frequency-domain methods construct amplitude and phase spectra from parameterized source and path models, then convert to the time domain.^[WEB:https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017] Scenario scaling differs fundamentally—it doesn't simulate a new waveform from source parameters but instead adapts an existing recorded template by amplitude adjustment alone, preserving the original frequency spectrum, duration, envelope, and phase characteristics.\n\nThe critical limitation of peak-component scaling is that the template record's frequency content reflects the original blast event's source characteristics, propagation path, and site conditions, not those of the scenario being modeled. Blast vibration frequency varies systematically with charge geometry, maximum instantaneous charge weight, slant distance, and geological structure. The temporal structure of a blast vibration record—its duration and envelope—depends on the delay pattern and timing of the original blast, with single-delay events producing simple pulses and multi-delay blasts creating composite waveforms. Since amplitude scaling preserves the ratio between all time samples, it doesn't change the temporal envelope, so if the template record has appropriate duration and envelope characteristics for the target scenario, scaling maintains those properties.\n\nHowever, when the three component time histories are scaled independently by different factors, the relative amplitudes between components shift, meaning the vector resultant of the scaled components no longer equals the template's vector resultant multiplied by a single factor—this creates potential inconsistency with predictions from resultant attenuation models.\n\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM] Additionally, the phase relationships between components are locked in by the template event and don't adjust to reflect the actual wave-type composition expected at the scenario's charge-distance combination, since different charge levels and distances would naturally excite different mixtures of P-waves, S-waves, and Rayleigh waves with distinct component polarizations that amplitude scaling alone cannot capture.\n\nNonlinear propagation effects in the near field—where material undergoes elastoplastic deformation and strain exceeds the linear elastic limit—aren't represented by empirical attenuation methods or scenario scaling, though physics-based source models using hydrodynamic codes can account for this behavior. Since empirical attenuation breaks down in the near field where measured peaks deviate from power-law forms, scenario scaling inherits these same limitations. position. The empirical power-law model doesn't capture anisotropic wave propagation from geological structure, directional effects from blast pattern orientation relative to joint sets, or topographic amplification and shadowing. Physics-based models can represent these effects explicitly using site characterization data, whereas empirical surfaces fitted to field data absorb all these influences implicitly into their regression constants, so when waveforms are scaled to targets from such surfaces, these confounding effects become inseparable from the scaling factor itself.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the draft for proper formatting—checking that footnotes are audited on each paragraph, citations are valid, no bullet points are used, LaTeX is properly formatted, and there's no instructional language. I should also add an introductory section and a title since this is a research manuscript file.\n\nI'm verifying the display math promotion rules—expressions with square roots, fractions, or integrals should be in display form, and I've already promoted the key equations like the PPV formula and the resultant velocity calculation correctly.\n\nNow I'm checking the section heading structure. The workflow calls for each slot to be a top-level section, so I'll use ## for the slot headings and # for the main document title.\n\nI'm doing a final pass to ensure every prose paragraph has an audit footnote attached.\n\nLooking at the third paragraph about empirical attenuation models, I need to be careful about how the display equation sits within the prose. According to the audit rules, a paragraph that ends with a colon before a display equation still counts as its own prose block and needs its own footnote—the equation doesn't absorb the footnote from what comes after it.\n\nSo I'm restructuring this to split the equation exposition properly: the lead-in claim gets its own footnote, then the display equation sits alone, then the interpretation paragraph that follows gets its own separate footnote. Same approach for the vector resultant equation. Let me reorganize these sections carefully as I finalize the manuscript. Now I'm mapping out the remaining slots with their paragraph structure—each section will have flowing prose blocks, each ending with a footnote, and I'll make sure the final slot trails off appropriately to match the pattern.\n\nI need to verify the DOIs for those key papers before finalizing the manuscript, particularly the USBM report and the Kostic reference, so I'm not citing sources without proper documentation. I'm following the citation preference hierarchy: DOI takes priority, then arXiv, then institutional web sources. I've confirmed the PLOS ONE DOI for Kostic et al. 2013, and I'm inferring DOIs from Springer URLs for the multivariate analysis and PCA papers based on their URL patterns, while noting that I lack verified DOIs for the ResearchGate paper and NIST publication. I can extract the DOI directly from that Springer URL as well. For the ScienceDirect papers where the DOI isn't readily available from the PII, I'll fall back to web citations. Same approach for the ResearchGate preprints—those will use web citations since they're author copies rather than published versions. I'm working through the DOI formats for these MDPI papers—the URL structure suggests a pattern, but I'm not confident enough in reconstructing the exact DOI, so I'll use web citations instead. Now I'm ready to write out the full manuscript, keeping it comprehensive while staying within the scope constraints around blast vibration literature.\n\nI notice the knowledge base manifest shows no pre-processed documents, which means I'll need to rely entirely on external research. The workflow requires me to flag this explicitly, so I'll note it at the beginning. The task specifies flowing paragraphs within each section rather than bullet points, so I'll structure the content accordingly while using headings for the overall document organization. I'll note upfront that the knowledge base has no source documents available, so I'm relying entirely on external research for this review. Now I'm ready to write the full manuscript with five flowing sections under the main headings—no subheadings within each slot, just continuous paragraphs covering the component attenuation and scaling gap analysis in blast vibration literature. time-series data and regulatory documents, confirming the standard practice of using the maximum component peak for compliance decisions while acknowledging that this approach discards directional information about how vibration energy is distributed across the three axes.\n\nThere's an important distinction between how peak resultant velocity can be calculated. The maximum component peak is simply the largest absolute value across any single axis, whereas the true resultant velocity at any moment is the vector magnitude combining all three components simultaneously. A third approach, the maximum vector sum, takes the square root of the sum of squares of the individual component peaks regardless of timing, which adds an extra safety margin but is now considered outdated because it produces unnecessarily conservative results. The component peaks occurring at different times means the true resultant and maximum vector sum produce different magnitudes, which directly impacts how we interpret attenuation data from empirical measurements. I should restructure this more clearly by separating the discussion of three-axis recording standards from the mathematical distinction between these peak definitions. I'm clarifying my audit approach: I'll base confidence levels on whether the citations I've already included actually support the claims, treating confirmed DOIs as high-confidence sources and search snippets more cautiously, while marking analytical claims as high-confidence if the logic checks out. Now I'm ready to write the complete manuscript efficiently.\n\nI also need to be careful about how I structure paragraphs with display equations—each lead-in claim before the math needs its own footnote, then the equation block stands alone, followed by an interpretation paragraph with another footnote. This keeps the citations properly anchored to their respective claims. where $SD$ is the scaled distance calculated 